{"version":1,"entries":{"Narrator: Javier was a nine-year-old boy who lived with his parents in the hillside town of Ademuz in the province of Valencia in Spain. Javier was a curious and energetic boy. His favorite pastime was to explore the hills and open fields around his town. The neighboring village of Riodeva, just 26 kilometers away from Ademuz, was famous because of a great discovery there. In 2003, paleontologists found the bones of the largest dinosaur in Europe, in a wheat field near the little village. When it was alive, this animal was over 38 meters long and had the weight of seven elephants. Javier liked dinosaurs more than anything else in the world. He also believed that there was an amazing dinosaur bone buried somewhere near his own town of Ademuz. His dream was to be the first person to find it. One summer morning, Javier was exploring in a field near a ravine just outside of the town. He looked up to see his two friends, Fernando and Pepe, running toward him with a colorful kite.\n\nFernando and Pepe: Let's fly it, Javier! they shouted.\n\nNarrator: Javier enjoyed playing with his friends. He quickly forgot about his exploring and grabbed the string of the kite. As he ran, the kite lifted into the sky. The boys cheered. Suddenly the kite spun around and dove into the ravine.\n\nJavier: I'll get it, shouted Javier.\n\nNarrator: He ran down into the ravine. As he reached for the kite, his foot caught on something. He fell to the rocky ground with a crash. His two friends ran up as he rubbed his head.\n\nPepe: Are you okay? Pepe asked.\n\nJavier: I think so, said Javier.\n\nNarrator: He looked down at a rough gray object sticking out of the ground.\n\nJavier: I tripped on that, he said.\n\nFernando: It looks like a rock, said Fernando.\n\nNarrator: Javier looked carefully at the object.\n\nJavier: I don't think it's a rock, he said excitedly. I think it's something else.\n\nPepe: Let's pull it out, said Pepe.\n\nJavier: No, said Javier. It's better to leave it right here.\n\nNarrator: Javier and the boys ran home to tell Javier's parents. Javier's father spoke to the mayor of the town who contacted the paleontology foundation. The next day, a paleontologist from the foundation came to the town. He followed Javier to the ravine and examined the boys' discovery.\n\nPaleontologist: It could be a dinosaur bone, he said. If it is, we'll need to determine how old it is.\n\nJavier: How do you do that? asked Javier.\n\nPaleontologist: This bone was buried in sedimentary rock which lies in layers under the ground. We'll look for volcanic ash below and above this rock layer. Through a special process, we can find out the ages of those layers of ash. Then we'll know that the dinosaur died sometime between those two ages.\n\nNarrator: Soon many paleontologists began excavating the area around the bone. They found more bones, as well as a skull. It was a unique dinosaur they didn't know about. Television reporters came to the town and spoke to Javier about the bone he discovered.\n\nReporter: This dinosaur will need a name, said one reporter. What do you think it should be called?\n\nPaleontologist: That's easy, said the man from the Paleontology Foundation. We'll call it the Javiersaurus":[{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/6b3fe0693d7c49bb7867.mp3","turn":0,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/407d14973ab207d029d9.mp3","turn":1,"speaker":"Fernando and Pepe"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/979b276ec5f0f3fe3a8f.mp3","turn":2,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/49bcdda191d508f6e691.mp3","turn":3,"speaker":"Javier"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/c8c32a72ed8a4c37f722.mp3","turn":4,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/8469f41e060bf3defc38.mp3","turn":5,"speaker":"Pepe"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/4fa5b424d1e12928ff06.mp3","turn":6,"speaker":"Javier"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/e563e7c7df26684f0ea2.mp3","turn":7,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/90b49103ca5e0313031b.mp3","turn":8,"speaker":"Javier"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/f54f1364a0233fb76544.mp3","turn":9,"speaker":"Fernando"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/55aaeed98d29f975d915.mp3","turn":10,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/b656fb9f3ceffd5ce8b1.mp3","turn":11,"speaker":"Javier"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/d87331a013b812ca0305.mp3","turn":12,"speaker":"Pepe"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/cf065a2d1c35f0edc797.mp3","turn":13,"speaker":"Javier"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/7f3dba27687dfd6e1d25.mp3","turn":14,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/47a2037ed017c4280cbc.mp3","turn":15,"speaker":"Paleontologist"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/567dfb00f9aad6004ae9.mp3","turn":16,"speaker":"Javier"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/c54a4249c94061884f7a.mp3","turn":17,"speaker":"Paleontologist"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/a823d29c0a6c13ff2581.mp3","turn":18,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/dac16530f958f57d4c5f.mp3","turn":19,"speaker":"Reporter"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/1acd9d9296709702a6a2.mp3","turn":20,"speaker":"Paleontologist"}],"Narrator: I'm making a special breakfast today: Granny's famous cinnamon buns.\n\nChild narrator: To make the best buns, you need the best ingredients.\n\nNarrator: I tell my sister Sam. So we hop on my bike and ride across town to Farmer Ray's.\n\nFarmer Ray: You've come to the right place. My chickens lay wonderful eggs.\n\nNarrator: The farmer says, pulling on his bushy beard. At the chicken coop, I gather two smooth brown eggs and a nervous chicken.\n\nFarmer Ray: Cinnamon buns, you say? If it's butter you're after, you'll need to pack your bags. The best butter I know comes from Denmark.\n\nNarrator: Farmer Ray smiles slyly. We all hop on his tractor, catch a plane, and watch as we zoom over the Atlantic Ocean. In Denmark, we head to rolling green fields, where we find an enormous sleepy cow. I introduce myself, my sister Sam, Farmer Ray, and the chicken to the cow's owner, explaining that my grandmother's cinnamon buns are not to be missed.\n\nFarmer Lena: Well, you've come to the right place. My butter is the creamiest in the world. Cinnamon buns, you say? If it's flour you need, the heartiest wheat I know comes from France.\n\nNarrator: Farmer Lena says, spilling some milk from a pail. We all ride the cow to the train to France, where we find an amber field of warm, dry grain and meet a French farmer who listens very closely to our plan.\n\nFarmer François: Well, you've come to the right place. My wheat makes the very best flour in the entire world.\n\nNarrator: Farmer François says, twirling his mustache. I grab two armfuls.\n\nFarmer François: Cinnamon buns, you say? If it's vanilla you need, the finest vanilla in all the world comes from Madagascar. Come with me.\n\nNarrator: We all hop in his hot-air balloon and set off over Africa, looking down on a magnificent leopard roaming the plains. In Madagascar, we march through the lush hills. There we meet Sabine, a vanilla grower, and I tell her the plan.\n\nFarmer Sabine: Well, if you're making famous cinnamon buns, you've come to the right place. My vines produce the most fragrant vanilla beans in all the world.\n\nNarrator: Farmer Sabine says, her eyes sparkling. I scoop up a handful of the heavy brown vanilla beans.\n\nFarmer Sabine: Madagascar grows sugar cane and cinnamon, too. Let me take you to my friend Patrick's plantation.\n\nNarrator: Patrick listens to the plan.\n\nPatrick: True, I have the sweetest sugar cane and the very best cinnamon in the world. Sugar cane I can give you, but I'm afraid I can't help you with cinnamon. I'm all out. We harvested it yesterday.\n\nChildren: No cinnamon for the cinnamon buns? Now what?\n\nNarrator: Something high atop a tree catches my eye: a small area of bark. I climb the tree and peel some off.\n\nChild narrator: This will do!\n\nNarrator: I say, grinning at the relieved crowd. We coax the cow and the chicken onto a steamship and head home before the ingredients spoil, mixing up a batch of Granny's famous cinnamon buns along the way.\n\nChild narrator: Well?\n\nNarrator: I ask as everyone chews and smiles.\n\nFarmers: You were right! These are truly the very best cinnamon buns in all the world.\n\nChild narrator: Now what should I make for lunch?":[{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/0d27772606fb42a7a47b.mp3","turn":0,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/d354c37627496fdc235c.mp3","turn":1,"speaker":"Child narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/650b38c97a9917034281.mp3","turn":2,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/4c2c649dce62e32f0acb.mp3","turn":3,"speaker":"Farmer Ray"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/24c3ea016a21253bc3d3.mp3","turn":4,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/a6cb4a235e08da717e7a.mp3","turn":5,"speaker":"Farmer Ray"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/0f1440f76dab2164174a.mp3","turn":6,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/b4c1e6802fd2f7c8b962.mp3","turn":7,"speaker":"Farmer Lena"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/da58a5412d8d3bc240ff.mp3","turn":8,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/8ce8a5109a17a9d0fa4f.mp3","turn":9,"speaker":"Farmer François"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/210693a061ea5486b87d.mp3","turn":10,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/fb2ee80915b49c018635.mp3","turn":11,"speaker":"Farmer François"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/719029d946b8350ac920.mp3","turn":12,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/ba82bcb61d8855c0c30c.mp3","turn":13,"speaker":"Farmer Sabine"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/5e6bcc6334f56f56e99b.mp3","turn":14,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/2543f676a63269d1a119.mp3","turn":15,"speaker":"Farmer Sabine"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/07e33f31db694bf7110c.mp3","turn":16,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/fcae76b2c81bdd00a145.mp3","turn":17,"speaker":"Patrick"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/4d0aed8bf2c4bf5dddc5.mp3","turn":18,"speaker":"Children"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/2d40e5c0cd28b1b3a324.mp3","turn":19,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/59aa03b2796cb5deb027.mp3","turn":20,"speaker":"Child narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/30b156f91aff34e290be.mp3","turn":21,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/db29e35b931848c0ce72.mp3","turn":22,"speaker":"Child narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/49f21674f1e04140fd4f.mp3","turn":23,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/36351a50dda53618d890.mp3","turn":24,"speaker":"Farmers"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/3732a2b227695b2984d1.mp3","turn":25,"speaker":"Child narrator"}],"Narrator: Can anyone make art? Can artists make art from anything? Let's look at art through the eyes of some very inspiring modern artists to answer these questions. The Painting Prodigy When he was five, Kieron Williamson drew sketches of boats he saw on a family trip to Cornwall, England. This was the start of Kieron's love of drawing. The more he drew, the more complex his drawings became. Kieron wanted to learn more about art, so he spent time with local artists in his home town of Norfolk, England. They helped him build his skills in drawing and painting. He now creates pictures using oil paints, pastels, and watercolors. Today, nine-year-old Kieron is considered to be a child prodigy. Around the world, people admire his beautiful artwork, including many famous people.\n\nKieron Williamson: The young artist says,I like creating art because it's fun and inspiring. It makes me think of places I can't see.\n\nNarrator: Art under your feet The next time you walk outside, you may discover that you are part of Edgar Mueller's art. Edgar is an expert street painter from Germany.\n\nEdgar Mueller: I always have the wish to create something new, he says.\n\nNarrator: You can find his paintings all around the world at festivals and on public walkways. His three-dimensional paintings include waterfalls, canyons, and more. Mueller uses washable paint or chalk, a variety of paint brushes, and string. His work helps people to look at a place differently. Stick sculptures Patrick Dougherty is a unique sculptor. He combines his love of nature with his skills as a carpenter. His goal is to build a great sculpture that excites people's imaginations. You can see his large-scale sculptures in many places around the world. Dougherty's sculptures are made of young trees or saplings. The saplings are flexible, so he can bend them into rounded shapes. He keeps them together by weaving the saplings around each other. Building the sculpture is a big job, so the local community helps Dougherty with his project. Many volunteers help to collect the saplings, mark out the space, and build the sculpture. The sculptures are temporary. They will decompose or break down over time. Painting the Universe When you look at the artwork of Korean artist Sung Hee Cho up close, you see small pieces of brightly colored paper on a large canvas. It may seem disorderly with colors scattered around. However, as you step back, you can imagine the stars, galaxies, and the beauty of the Universe. Cho combines Hanji, traditional Korean paper, with paint. First, she paints the canvas. Then, she cuts out pieces of Hanji into small shapes such as flower petals. Next, she dyes and paints the small pieces of paper in a different color. Then, she glues thousands of them onto the canvas in layers to capture the glimmer of light. She repeats this process over and over again. She wants to create artwork that is as magical as stars. Inspiring artists come from every part of the world. Each one inspires us in a different way. By creating and sharing their art, they help us appreciate and understand the world around us.":[{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/095eb3ae0e4e11fa0901.mp3","turn":0,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/6654988d0f12c0200617.mp3","turn":1,"speaker":"Kieron Williamson"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/f824241455bbb84c74bc.mp3","turn":2,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/f95838baebf14847b142.mp3","turn":3,"speaker":"Edgar Mueller"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/029d9f2fd5dfb08d157e.mp3","turn":4,"speaker":"Narrator"}],"Narrator: Each day after school, when other boys play soccer, practice for the school play, or ride bikes, Theo goes to the art museum. Everyone he knows thinks this is strange, even his own family.\n\nMom: Don't you want to play a sport, or be in the play with all of your friends? I will sign you up for something.\n\nNarrator: His mother offers. Theo does not tell his mother that he has no friends at school. His friends are all here, in the museum. They are on every wall, in brilliant colors and oil paints and pastels. The paintings are Theo's friends. Today, Theo rushes to get to the museum after school, because he will be meeting some new friends. An exhibition is opening, The Mountain Landscapes of Zayan Kahn. Inside the brightly lit gallery, there are ten new paintings, each in a wooden frame. Each painting features a different mountain. Some of the mountains are covered in snow, while others are green or rocky. Theo sits on a bench and looks at one painting for a very long time. He likes the contrast of the heavy black lines of the mountain and the pale blue sky. Theo takes out his notebook and his pencil and begins to make a sketch. People come and go from the small gallery, but Theo sits for a long time, making sketch after sketch. After a while, there is only one other person left in the gallery. He is sitting on a bench on the other side of the little room. Theo decides to ignore the man.\n\nTheo (thinking): I don't want to talk to him.\n\nNarrator: The man stands and walks toward the exit, but he stops next to Theo. Theo's sketch is of a mountain from very far away. The mountain is small and surrounded by space.\n\nZayan Kahn: That's an interesting perspective.\n\nNarrator: The man comments. Theo is shocked. He never shows his artwork to anyone because he worries that it isn't any good.\n\nTheo: Shall I show you some more sketches?\n\nNarrator: He asks. The man smiles and nods. Theo hands the man his notebook and sits quietly as the man turns the pages.\n\nZayan Kahn: I like the way you create texture with shading. I like to do that, too.\n\nNarrator: The man remarks. Theo notices the man's hands and fingers are stained with paint. In his jacket pocket, there are several colored pencils and a paintbrush. He is holding an old tattered sketchbook with his name written on the front.\n\nTheo: You are Zayan Kahn!\n\nNarrator: Theo exclaims, astounded that he is talking to the famous artist. Zayan Kahn looks so much younger than Theo imagined, and he is so kind and friendly.\n\nZayan Kahn: That's right.\n\nTheo: I'm Theo. One day, I want to be an artist like you.\n\nNarrator: Theo tells him. Zayan Kahn shakes his head.\n\nZayan Kahn: No, don't say that.\n\nNarrator: He says. Theo looks down.\n\nTheo: Oh.\n\nNarrator: He says. Zayan Kahn points to Theo's notebook.\n\nZayan Kahn: You are already an artist, Theo.\n\nNarrator: He says. Theo is speechless with happiness. He does not say anything else, and neither does Zayan Kahn. They just sit quietly in the room, sketching, two artists, surrounded by friends.":[{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/ac830d788a112017a3d0.mp3","turn":0,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/c7c6ae368b8cf4c0c17b.mp3","turn":1,"speaker":"Mom"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/0956b8d877b7bf3373d1.mp3","turn":2,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/ccf670bc8b798c5f065c.mp3","turn":3,"speaker":"Theo (thinking)"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/2344f63da5a187bb87c1.mp3","turn":4,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/cd7de97809f2a9dce9f5.mp3","turn":5,"speaker":"Zayan Kahn"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/91379dbde17010d88bbf.mp3","turn":6,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/23563c434126b993b1d7.mp3","turn":7,"speaker":"Theo"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/976a44b6b349f76c273b.mp3","turn":8,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/bf79201de23947ec92ce.mp3","turn":9,"speaker":"Zayan Kahn"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/1f234d68e01b15b8f5d7.mp3","turn":10,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/b32e5cbbd1a1b9084787.mp3","turn":11,"speaker":"Theo"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/1564ec4c60d7c2036022.mp3","turn":12,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/60abf893dd8af2e91e7a.mp3","turn":13,"speaker":"Zayan Kahn"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/24f01ca8d7e4561a0c4a.mp3","turn":14,"speaker":"Theo"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/2da0e4c05f4f6174e402.mp3","turn":15,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/b80f6cfdd825d5efe417.mp3","turn":16,"speaker":"Zayan Kahn"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/1711bf2f134341dc83c9.mp3","turn":17,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/b7b0aa761da10f191de5.mp3","turn":18,"speaker":"Theo"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/90f25744e8efa01a0f48.mp3","turn":19,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/13b6106899fdc56f0427.mp3","turn":20,"speaker":"Zayan Kahn"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/ebb52d0dec3c620dbca6.mp3","turn":21,"speaker":"Narrator"}],"Narrator: Marcy was nervous. She was the winner of the Mayor for a Day contest at her school. For one day, she would be the mayor of her city. Marcy's mother drove her to the Glenview City Hall.\n\nMom: You'll probably walk around with the mayor, her mother said. Watch what he does and take notes, you'll learn a lot.\n\nNarrator: Outside City Hall, Marcy met Mayor Wilson. To her surprise, he was holding a beach ball.\n\nMayor Wilson: Congratulations, Marcy, he said. Keep the city running smoothly. You'll work hard, won't you?\n\nNarrator: Marcy nodded nervously.\n\nMarcy: I'll do my best, sir.\n\nMayor Wilson: Then I'm going to the beach. Mr. Clark will be your assistant today. Goodbye.\n\nNarrator: It was a busy morning. Mr. Clark took Marcy to the new city library, which was about to open for the first time. Marcy cut a celebration ribbon and made a short speech.\n\nMarcy: Citizens of Glenview read books every day, she declared. Reading makes you smarter.\n\nNarrator: As the crowd clapped, Mr. Clark tapped on his watch.\n\nMr. Clark: You have a meeting in ten minutes with the president of the Terrific Toy Company. We want them to build a factory here in Glenview. Many people could get jobs there.\n\nMarcy: Let's go, said Marcy.\n\nNarrator: In the mayor's office, Marcy spent one hour talking to Hilda Hanson, the president of Terrific Toys.\n\nMarcy: I promise you that the people of Glenview will build interesting and creative toys, Marcy said.\n\nHilda Hanson: For such a young mayor, you impress me, said Hilda. We'll start building our factory here tomorrow.\n\nNarrator: As Hilda drove away, Mr. Clark ran up to Marcy.\n\nMr. Clark: The garbage collectors are unhappy, he exclaimed. There is too much garbage to pick up. They want more workers. What will we do?\n\nNarrator: Marcy realized that she wasn't nervous anymore. She thought for a moment and then snapped her fingers.\n\nMarcy: We'll start a recycling program, she said. That will reduce garbage, they won't need more workers.\n\nMr. Clark: Great idea, said Mr. Clark.\n\nNarrator: After lunch, Mr. Clark ran into Marcy's office.\n\nMr. Clark: You have a city council meeting in five minutes, he shouted.\n\nNarrator: At the meeting, the head of the city council looked worried.\n\nCity council head: The playground equipment at the city park needs fresh paint, she said. The park needs new flowers, but we have no money for extra workers.\n\nMarcy: Do we have money for ice cream? Marcy asked.\n\nNarrator: The city council members looked confused.\n\nMarcy: I will have a news conference today, Marcy explained. Next Saturday will be our first Glenview volunteer day. People can volunteer at the park and enjoy free ice cream. The citizens of our city will paint and plant and get the job done.\n\nCouncil members: Wonderful idea, Mayor Marcy, the members cheered.\n\nNarrator: At 5.30, Mr. Clark thanked Marcy for her hard work. They walked to the front of City Hall, where Marcy's mother was waiting. Marcy got in the car and stretched her legs.\n\nMom: Did you have fun, Marcy?\n\nMarcy: I did.\n\nMom: Who knows, said her mother, maybe you'll be a real mayor someday.\n\nMarcy: Marcy smiled, maybe I will, she said.":[{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/f6940412033073b69e45.mp3","turn":0,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/c15bb895436318321b30.mp3","turn":1,"speaker":"Mom"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/46060326fdabf9c71d0b.mp3","turn":2,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/b38f78ef3cd3b2331579.mp3","turn":3,"speaker":"Mayor Wilson"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/2992089bcc3f0e5c9888.mp3","turn":4,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/169ccac49f29f745a10e.mp3","turn":5,"speaker":"Marcy"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/120d3ee5eb5723aaf24d.mp3","turn":6,"speaker":"Mayor Wilson"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/19efa363eb440083d5e3.mp3","turn":7,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/8e8d0bd6c7828b0a59dd.mp3","turn":8,"speaker":"Marcy"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/2090b621db7ba0601f2d.mp3","turn":9,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/ecd5cfc45c1f51785fa7.mp3","turn":10,"speaker":"Mr. Clark"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/fb16d8e1db48902f0d96.mp3","turn":11,"speaker":"Marcy"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/fee1eba007a18c3a504a.mp3","turn":12,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/4eabb6c08dbbb6c01a29.mp3","turn":13,"speaker":"Marcy"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/c9c099a3611de75bae37.mp3","turn":14,"speaker":"Hilda Hanson"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/b83f9e0af6c06fb0f683.mp3","turn":15,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/cdc3144c18760a268e7d.mp3","turn":16,"speaker":"Mr. Clark"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/8f93d24984280a6c577c.mp3","turn":17,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/b7702a3e76f4fa691998.mp3","turn":18,"speaker":"Marcy"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/1cf81a32c5e0c10ad4fa.mp3","turn":19,"speaker":"Mr. Clark"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/d12c9ee5a49d283a7bb4.mp3","turn":20,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/cdebecfc641319cf2257.mp3","turn":21,"speaker":"Mr. Clark"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/a3b261733c4f19f17c8a.mp3","turn":22,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/c32caeeaf2c5ed1aab42.mp3","turn":23,"speaker":"City council head"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/95dad3ed341c65245d0c.mp3","turn":24,"speaker":"Marcy"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/6d39a187e25b4fcacc7f.mp3","turn":25,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/02c3179992039bdbafbd.mp3","turn":26,"speaker":"Marcy"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/7e9416c39a3985de5ead.mp3","turn":27,"speaker":"Council members"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/bb3d93e0fa359becc233.mp3","turn":28,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/71ca2c84f127754336e7.mp3","turn":29,"speaker":"Mom"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/31c0930b88019fb5ef85.mp3","turn":30,"speaker":"Marcy"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/0e9552549811b266e083.mp3","turn":31,"speaker":"Mom"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/528908872c50d154b2ef.mp3","turn":32,"speaker":"Marcy"}],"Narrator: Thanks to the mass media, news travels fast. Journalist Meghan Michaels interviews Dr. Philip Kendall, a mass media expert.\n\nMeghan Michaels: Thank you for meeting with me, Dr. Kendall.\n\nDr. Kendall: You're welcome. People like to share news, don't they?\n\nMeghan Michaels: Absolutely.\n\nDr. Kendall: Long ago, when early humans discovered how to make fire, they told their friends. Then those friends told others. Ideas spread from person to person, and in fact, they still do. However, mass media is different. It brings news and knowledge to many people very quickly.\n\nMeghan Michaels: When did mass media begin?\n\nDr. Kendall: It began around 1440, when Johannes Gutenberg changed the way news travelled. You see, he invented the printing press. Before the printing press, people had to make copies of books by hand. Suddenly, this invention could make copies of books much more easily. Then, about 150 years later, people printed the first newspapers in Europe. Today, people around the world read newspapers every day.\n\nMeghan Michaels: How many newspapers are there?\n\nDr. Kendall: Newspapers, along with magazines, are very popular. There are over 6,000 daily newspapers in the world today. Publishers print almost 400 million copies each day. Magazines are popular because they focus on one subject, such as news, movies, fashion, or sports. Advertising helps to pay for the cost of publishing newspapers and magazines.\n\nMeghan Michaels: So what happened next in the history of mass media?\n\nDr. Kendall: Movies were the next form of mass media. People started going to movie theatres about 100 years ago. These new moving pictures amazed people. Often, movie theatres showed newsreels, too. These were short films with news from around the world. People watched the news for the first time this way.\n\nMeghan Michaels: And then the television came next, right?\n\nDr. Kendall: Actually, the radio came next. It appeared about the same time as movies, but the first radio station didn't get started until about 1920. Today, there are over 44,000 radio stations around the world.\n\nMeghan Michaels: Do people still get their news from the radio?\n\nDr. Kendall: Yes, they do. Many radio stations report the news every hour. Some stations broadcast the news all the time. People can listen to the news while they drive or work.\n\nMeghan Michaels: When did the television appear?\n\nDr. Kendall: Television broadcasting started in the 1930s. Now, television is everywhere, bringing news and entertainment to people around the world. Some stations now offer 24-hour news. Events are broadcast live while they are happening.\n\nMeghan Michaels: So people have a lot of choices for their news, don't they?\n\nDr. Kendall: They do. But you mustn't forget the latest choice, the web. Today, almost one third of the people in the world use the web, and this number will continue to grow. The web, or internet, lets us read news, watch videos, and share information through social networks and blogs. Many people think that the web is now the most important form of mass media. Also, smartphones give us web service, so you don't have to carry a computer to check the news. We live in very interesting times.\n\nMeghan Michaels: That's true. It will be exciting to see what's next in the world of mass media.":[{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/b4c6db29bc294a57d1ab.mp3","turn":0,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/132a4f85b3a00954ae13.mp3","turn":1,"speaker":"Meghan Michaels"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/309cd1d4e91523ac2dec.mp3","turn":2,"speaker":"Dr. Kendall"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/17ddc1f38afc26828670.mp3","turn":3,"speaker":"Meghan Michaels"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/6df8f38b87a781865aaa.mp3","turn":4,"speaker":"Dr. Kendall"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/d6f673f80c348a6255da.mp3","turn":5,"speaker":"Meghan Michaels"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/ceea88c3c0e971db2f4d.mp3","turn":6,"speaker":"Dr. Kendall"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/ef57d245f76156377ecd.mp3","turn":7,"speaker":"Meghan Michaels"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/85403d537c978c7f10ae.mp3","turn":8,"speaker":"Dr. Kendall"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/c5a2f14464f52d50a05a.mp3","turn":9,"speaker":"Meghan Michaels"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/8dc71c5b24700f4d6689.mp3","turn":10,"speaker":"Dr. Kendall"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/59f96eb0e2e6f5620a24.mp3","turn":11,"speaker":"Meghan Michaels"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/85bb663ab0e5e0b293c0.mp3","turn":12,"speaker":"Dr. Kendall"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/c2be682c0483520e10db.mp3","turn":13,"speaker":"Meghan Michaels"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/537c86155114f916fdca.mp3","turn":14,"speaker":"Dr. Kendall"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/5a766f1a84bcc2275c07.mp3","turn":15,"speaker":"Meghan Michaels"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/0ec437bdf9d0dc677b89.mp3","turn":16,"speaker":"Dr. Kendall"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/1e437f538c996f95b19d.mp3","turn":17,"speaker":"Meghan Michaels"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/b49067f375fb6a6bd154.mp3","turn":18,"speaker":"Dr. Kendall"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/450a8a5432e8fdc509dd.mp3","turn":19,"speaker":"Meghan Michaels"}],"Elsa (blog): July 5th, I have decided to keep a blog so the world can read about all the things I investigate. Today, I heard that they are going to tear down the old stone house across the street. The newspaper says the city government owns the house, and just one person lives there. Why does the city government own an old house? Who lives there? I have decided to find out because I want to save the house. July 6th, I use the internet to search for articles about old houses on my street. I found an entire website called the Lost History of Stone House. It said the stone building was once the gatehouse of a huge mansion. A family called the Martins lived there, until one night, a terrible fire burned the mansion down. The young daughter of a maid smelled the smoke and woke up every single person in the house. Everyone got out safely that night. The website showed the front page of an old newspaper with a story about the fire. The headline said,Young girl, 9, saves seven people in mysterious fire. She was the same age as me. July 7th, I am going to visit the house to see if I can talk to the person who lives there. I have never done an interview before, and I am very nervous. What if I make a mistake? July 7th, part two, I did it. A woman who looks like my grandmother answered the door. She said her name was Miss Lu, and that she would be happy to answer my questions. She knew all about the mansion and the fire. I learned that the Martin family donated a lot of money to the city to help build the library and the hospital. After the fire, they decided to move away and let the city have their land. I asked Miss Lu if she knew the Martins. She smiled and said no one had ever asked her that before. When she told me the answer, I couldn't believe it. My mom says I have to go to sleep now, so read my blog tomorrow for the rest of the story. July 8th, I'm back. Miss Lu knew the Martins because she lived in the mansion too. Yes, readers. Miss Lu was the little girl who saved the family that night. I am writing a letter to the editor of our newspaper, asking the mayor not to destroy the house. Miss Lu is a hero, and it isn't fair to take away her home. July 10th, today a television reporter came to interview me. You can watch it on channel seven tonight. July 11th, I have more wonderful news. My story will be on a radio show that will be broadcast to the entire city tonight. July 15th, today I got an email from the mayor. He said there will be a special meeting to discuss the house. I'm amazed that he took the time to write to me. August 1st, awesome news. The mayor decided not to tear down the old house. He agrees that Miss Lu is a hero, and she'll be able to live there as long as she likes. This is the first news story I have ever investigated. I love being a reporter, and I can't wait to find more stories.":[{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/e04394983f5276d933c5.mp3","turn":0,"speaker":"Elsa (blog)"}],"Narrator: The state of Quintana Roo, in Mexico's Yucatan Peninsula, has experienced many hurricanes. Luis Sandoval lived in the capital city of Chetumal when Hurricane Dean hit the coast. He was nine years old. On August 17, 2007, Luis Sandoval was eating a sandwich after school when the phone rang. It was his father, who worked for the city government in Chetumal.\n\nDad: Luis, is your mother there? he asked.\n\nLuis: No, Papa. She went to the supermarket with Pablo and Alex.\n\nDad: Please listen carefully. Hurricane Dean is moving in our direction. It will reach our coast in three or four days. The government has declared a state of emergency.\n\nNarrator: Luis's heart started to beat quickly.\n\nLuis: Are we in danger, Papa?\n\nDad: Not if we prepare. It may be as bad as Hurricane Wilma. Do you remember what we did then?\n\nLuis: Yes, Papa.\n\nDad: Then start preparing. All of you need to leave Chetumal and go further inland tomorrow morning. I will have to stay here.\n\nNarrator: His father hung up. Luis remembered Hurricane Wilma very well. A rise in the ocean, called a storm surge, flooded large areas along the coast. Almost 600 millimeters of rain fell in many places. Waves as high as eight meters smashed violently into the third floors of hotels. Winds up to 240 kilometers per hour destroyed homes. Now the state of Quintana Roo was prepared for Hurricane Dean. The government was ready to help people leave the area. For those who couldn't leave, there were 530 local storm shelters, with food, blankets and medicine. However, families also had to protect themselves. Luis checked the emergency list in the storage room and gathered the following items. 20 liters of bottled water. One large box of dry and canned food. One small radio with batteries. One first aid kit. Two flashlights. Three boxes of tissue paper. Four sleeping bags. Emergency cash. Luis took out two small backpacks for his little brothers. They contained some clothes and were easy to carry. He took out two large backpacks for his mother and himself. He put some of the food, water and supplies in each one. The rest of the supplies would go in the trunk of the car. Suddenly, Luis's mother appeared in the doorway. His little brothers Pablo and Alex were holding her hands tightly.\n\nMom: Your father called me, his mother said. I tried to buy food and water, but they turned us away at the supermarket. There were too many people there.\n\nLuis: Don't worry, Mama, said Luis,I've already packed everything we need.\n\nMom: Good, said his mother,we have storm shutters, but you and I have to help Aunt Alma put boards over her windows tonight. We'll leave early tomorrow morning.\n\nNarrator: The next morning, Luis, his mother, his aunt and his brothers left the city. They drove many kilometers inland and stayed at a storm shelter. They would be safe from the hurricane there. Luis's father remained in Chetumal, helping those who could not leave the city. Hurricane Dean made landfall near the town of Majahual on Tuesday, August 21, 2007. It was a Category 5 storm, with winds as high as 320 kilometers per hour. The high winds and water destroyed Majahual. Parts of Chetumal, 65 kilometers south, had floods. However, thanks to the excellent preparations by the government and the people of Quintana Ro, not one life was lost on the Yucatan Peninsula.":[{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/fe864a559248b3543b71.mp3","turn":0,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/38f7abeb67015b818a10.mp3","turn":1,"speaker":"Dad"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/d0a2c073e5f8bf923ef8.mp3","turn":2,"speaker":"Luis"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/542c61349fb6bee373fd.mp3","turn":3,"speaker":"Dad"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/24dbe3e03d581441abbf.mp3","turn":4,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/99d307ce8fb7772c54dc.mp3","turn":5,"speaker":"Luis"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/8e1fc43296f8cb8d1395.mp3","turn":6,"speaker":"Dad"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/8df0f0c4fe0f7a47ceaf.mp3","turn":7,"speaker":"Luis"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/7ba7238e4d2e19c3c7d5.mp3","turn":8,"speaker":"Dad"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/d105deba95cbae315c72.mp3","turn":9,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/f5b414aae63f34c9d993.mp3","turn":10,"speaker":"Mom"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/317201aa7ba7a24f0ea1.mp3","turn":11,"speaker":"Luis"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/e5764a8ca6a23c582b2e.mp3","turn":12,"speaker":"Mom"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/6403cc06508525a7fca2.mp3","turn":13,"speaker":"Narrator"}],"Narrator: Camila lived in Belize City on the coast of Belize in Central America. Her father worked for the government's fisheries department, and he helped to manage the seven marine parks off the coast of the country. One day, Camila's father invited her to visit one of the parks with him.\n\nDad: I'm going to Glover's Reef this weekend, he explained. Would you like to join me?\n\nCamila: Of course, she said.\n\nNarrator: During their flight to the coastal town of Dangriga, Camila's father explained that Glover's Reef was on the edge of the Belize Barrier Reef, one of the largest coral reef systems in the world. Hundreds of fish and coral species lived in these reefs. He said that many coral reefs around the world were dying from pollution and overfishing. From Dangriga, Camila and her father took a two-hour boat ride to Glover's Reef. Camila's excitement grew as they neared a small island with its beautiful coastline and turquoise water.\n\nDad: We'll snorkel through the park tomorrow, her father said.\n\nNarrator: The next morning, Camila and her father put on their goggles and entered the blue waters of the park. The beauty and variety of the underwater world astonished Camila. Hundreds of butterfly fish, parrot fish, and seahorses darted around her. She watched sea turtles and manta rays glide among the rainbow-colored coral. Camila saw how the plants and animals of the reef live together perfectly. She took many pictures with her father's underwater camera. It was the most exciting experience of her life. As they left the water, Camila and her father heard a loud noise. They turned and saw a large speedboat filled with tourists racing toward the reef area.\n\nCamila: They'll scare the fish, shouted Camila.\n\nDad: There's nothing we can do, said her father. They're just outside of the park border.\n\nNarrator: Camila watched the boat continue along the border and then speed away. She was angry and upset. At lunch, Camila was very quiet. When she finished eating, she turned to her father.\n\nCamila: I'm going to do something for our planet's coral reefs, she said. We need to help them.\n\nNarrator: Back in Belize City, Camila spoke to her class about coral reefs.\n\nCamila (class talk): In Glover's Reef, I learned that coral reefs are an important part of our ocean biome because they are homes for hundreds of kinds of animals and plants. They are wonderful places to see the beauty of our oceans close up. My father taught me that coral reefs keep our shores safe from powerful waves and that scientists can find new medicines from plants and animals that live there.\n\nNarrator: When she got home, Camila created a petition on the internet.\n\nCamila (petition): It Read Governments of the world, please help the ocean's coral reefs. Keep our coral reefs safe and healthy for everyone.\n\nNarrator: In three weeks, 20,000 people in 38 countries signed Camila's petition. Within one year, three countries passed laws to protect their coral reefs.\n\nDad: You have made a difference, Camila, said her father. Thank you. The coral reefs of the world, thank you, too.":[{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/2870cf89508978ebe3bf.mp3","turn":0,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/26644505c8358484b951.mp3","turn":1,"speaker":"Dad"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/03c7221b3164814145e5.mp3","turn":2,"speaker":"Camila"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/64a3a7d243dbc7588aec.mp3","turn":3,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/644b04a495be2f9f723d.mp3","turn":4,"speaker":"Dad"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/a85003d903cc0963d983.mp3","turn":5,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/e83c6d645dae5183953c.mp3","turn":6,"speaker":"Camila"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/a4f0f0eb0b13f5b2c9a6.mp3","turn":7,"speaker":"Dad"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/8d915ccf8de2f8ffa799.mp3","turn":8,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/764f20499346fc7c5244.mp3","turn":9,"speaker":"Camila"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/395be5553a5a21e61fba.mp3","turn":10,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/62ea069815078740b8cd.mp3","turn":11,"speaker":"Camila (class talk)"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/612afb8609fb94f26170.mp3","turn":12,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/ba72a5669ba967232df3.mp3","turn":13,"speaker":"Camila (petition)"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/be203b01ea954fc7f001.mp3","turn":14,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/9feb5007bb8075f9c844.mp3","turn":15,"speaker":"Dad"}]}}